Q: Call a function periodically without using threads and the sleep() method in C A: <p>The most accurate solution in my tests is from the following code:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;time.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/timerfd.h&gt;

void *
say_hello()
{
    int rc, fd;
    struct itimerspec myitime;
    int64_t buffer;

    fd = timerfd_create(CLOCK_REALTIME, 0);

    // Every three seconds
    myitime.it_interval.tv_sec = 3;
    myitime.it_interval.tv_nsec = 0;

    // Start at the NEXT full second
    rc = clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &amp;(myitime.it_value));
    myitime.it_value.tv_sec += 1;
    myitime.it_value.tv_nsec = 0;

    rc = timerfd_settime(fd, TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME, &amp;myitime, NULL);

    // Wait for input from 'fd', then print hello
    while(true) {
        read(fd, &amp;buffer, 8);
        printf(&quot;Hello\n&quot;);
    }

    return NULL;
}

int main()
{
    say_hello();

    // Never returns
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Of course, <code>say_hello</code> can be spawned as a thread.</p>
<p>The <code>timerfs</code> call, on my unloaded system produces a printer output with a 5.3 microsecond <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RMS</a> variation. The <code>evtimer</code> method produces an output with a 319 microsecond RMS variation.</p>

Q: Asynchronous request and insertion of received nodes A: <p>It seems to me that you want a function to create an HTML element with some synchronously determined attributes (like the <code>className</code> or <code>textContent</code>) and some asynchronously determined children (for example, the result of HTTP requests).</p>
<p>Here is a simplified working example that illustrates what I mean. It creates a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element with synchronously determined <code>textContent</code> &quot;Your IP address&quot; and adds one child, which is another <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element whose <code>textContent</code> is your IP address as determined by an HTTP request.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function el(t, p, ...c) {
  const elem = document.createElement(t);
  Object.assign(elem, p);
  Promise.all(c).then(function(children) {
    for (const c of children)
      elem.appendChild(c);
  });
  return elem;
}
document.body.appendChild(el(
  "div",
  {textContent: "Your IP address"},
  fetch("https://httpbin.org/ip")
  .then(
    r =&gt; r.json()
  ).then(
    j =&gt; el(
      "div",
      {textContent: j.origin}
    )
  )
));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><code>Promise.all</code> is used to ensure that the children are added in the same order in which they are given in the argument list, despite their asynchronousness.</p>
<p>Is this what you want to achieve?</p>

Q: Nest.js Winston, some logs not sending to Loki A: <p>There are two separate issues causing this.</p>
<h2>1. Loki rejects the payload with a 4xx when <code>error</code> level is logged</h2>
<p>When <code>NestJS Logger</code> calls <code>error()</code>, <code>nest-winston</code> puts the context (or stack trace) in a <code>stack</code> field as an array, producing something like <code>{&quot;stack&quot;: [&quot;TemCleanerJob&quot;]}</code>. Loki rejects the entire push request because it can't parse that array as a valid log line value, returning a 4xx error. You won't see it anywhere because <code>onConnectionError</code> only fires on connection-level errors, not on HTTP error responses. Github issue <a href="https://github.com/JaniAnttonen/winston-loki/issues/188" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/JaniAnttonen/winston-loki/issues/188</a></p>
<p>The fix is to sanitize the <code>stack</code> field before it reaches Loki:</p>
<p>typescript</p>
<pre><code>const cleanForLoki = winston.format((info) =&gt; {
  if (Array.isArray(info.stack)) {
    info.stack = info.stack.filter(Boolean).join('\n') || undefined;
    if (!info.stack) delete info.stack;
  }
  return info;
});
</code></pre>
<h2>2. Default batching drops all logs in a failed batch</h2>
<p>With <code>batching: true</code> (the default), when Loki rejects a batch due to the above, <strong>all logs in that batch are silently dropped</strong>, not just the one that caused the error. That's why you lose <code>log</code> and <code>warn</code> too.</p>
<p>Fix: set <code>batching: false</code>.</p>
<h2>Final config</h2>
<p>typescript</p>
<pre><code>const cleanForLoki = winston.format((info) =&gt; {
  if (Array.isArray(info.stack)) {
    info.stack = info.stack.filter(Boolean).join('\n') || undefined;
    if (!info.stack) delete info.stack;
  }
  return info;
});

export const loggerConfig: winston.LoggerOptions = {
  level: 'verbose',
  transports: [
    new winston.transports.Console({
      format: winston.format.combine(
        winston.format.timestamp(),
        nestWinstonModuleUtilities.format.nestLike('NestApp', {
          prettyPrint: true,
        }),
      ),
    }),
    new LokiTransport({
      host: process.env.LOKI_HOST!,
      labels: { app: 'backend', env: process.env.NODE_ENV || 'development' },
      json: true,
      format: winston.format.combine(
        cleanForLoki(),
        winston.format.json(),
      ),
      replaceTimestamp: true,
      batching: false,
      onConnectionError: (err) =&gt; console.error(err),
    }),
  ],
};
</code></pre>
<p>The reason your <code>LoggerInterceptor</code> worked fine is that it only calls <code>this.logger.log()</code>, which never produces the <code>stack</code> array and never causes a batch failure.</p>

Q: Flutter:Do an operation if await takes more then 2 seconds A: <blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Flutter:Do an operation if await takes more then 2 seconds</p>
</blockquote>
</blockquote>
<p>After canceling the wait, you can also (if necessary) analyze the result of the awaited operation.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:async';

import 'package:multitasking/multitasking.dart';

Future&lt;void&gt; main(List&lt;String&gt; args) async {
  final cts = CancellationTokenSource(Duration(seconds: 2));
  final task = _doWork();
  try {
    final result = await task.withCancellation(cts.token);
    print('I am happy! Result is $result');
  } on TaskCanceledException {
    print('Waiting time expired');
    unawaited(() async {
      try {
        await task;
        if (task.result == 42) {
          print('Oh, I should have waited a little longer');
        }
      } catch (e) {/**/}
    }());
  }

  print('Done');
}

Task&lt;int&gt; _doWork() {
  return Task.run(() async {
    await Task.delay(5000);
    return 42;
  });
}

</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Waiting time expired
Done
Oh, I should have waited a little longer
</code></pre>
